  {
    slug: "978-openai-squarefree-quartics-refereed",
    problemNum: 978,
    title: "#978: n⁴+2 is squarefree infinitely often — OpenAI's proof that every irreducible quartic with no fixed square divisor takes squarefree values with the predicted density, its Lean replayed here (a shorter route than the paper's) and its paper read by AI seats; Formal Conjectures' three #978 statements proved in Lean from it",
    date: "2026-10-22",
    status: "proof_claimed",
    originatedByUs: false,
    summary:
      "Erdős wrote in 1953 that he could not prove n⁴+2 squarefree for infinitely many n, and in 1981 that there was no quartic polynomial about which anyone could. Hooley had the (d−1)-power-free case in 1967; at d−2 the record stopped at degree 9 (Browning 2011). OpenAI's preprint, dated 24 September and public since 6 October, claims the (d−2)-power-free values have the predicted Euler-product density for every irreducible f of degree d ≥ 4 with no fixed prime (d−2)-th-power divisor: n⁴+2 is squarefree for about 75.67% of n. We replayed its Lean (63 modules, Mathlib only) in the kernel: standard axioms, statement matched to the comparator — as far as we could find, the first reported replay. The Lean takes a shorter route than the paper (no determinant cuts, no quintic deletion) and proves degrees ≥ 9 itself, without Browning. Four AI seats read the paper: HOLDS AS READ on everything #978 needs, one fillable gap in a side corollary. Ours: Lean proofs of Formal Conjectures' three #978 statements, tagged solved there but still sorry, and two independent counts. The theorem is OpenAI's.",
    body: `
<p><strong>The problem.</strong> <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/978">Erdős problem #978</a> [Er53; Er65b, p. 219; Er81h, p. 178]. Erdős proved in 1953 that an irreducible integer polynomial of degree k ≥ 3 takes infinitely many (k−1)-power-free values (apart from a 2-adic exception when k is a power of 2), and Hooley proved in 1967 that it does so with positive density. One power lower, nothing was known for small degree. Erdős wrote in 1953: "I have also not been able to prove, for example, that n<sup>4</sup>+2 is squarefree for infinitely many n" (p. 425). In 1965: "I could prove nothing about the representation of (k − 2)-th power-free numbers, for example, I cannot show that n<sup>4</sup> + 2 represents infinitely many squarefree numbers" (§6, p. 219). In 1981: "there is no quartic polynomial about which we can prove that it represents infinitely many squarefree integers" (p. 178). The problem page asks: if k &gt; 3 and no prime p has p<sup>k−2</sup> dividing every value f(n), are there infinitely many n with f(n) (k−2)-power-free? In particular, is n<sup>4</sup>+2 squarefree infinitely often?</p>
<p>The local condition was added to the page on 31 March 2026, after Moritz Firsching posted a counterexample to the unconditioned question found by DeepMind's prover agent: x<sup>6</sup>+33x<sup>5</sup>+21x<sup>4</sup>+63x<sup>3</sup>+18x<sup>2</sup>+24x+48, which is congruent to x(x−1)⋯(x−5) mod 16 and so has 2<sup>4</sup> dividing every value. Before this release the record was:</p>
<ul>
<li>k ≥ (√2 − ½)d (Nair 1976), which reaches k = d−2 only for d ≥ 24;</li>
<li>k ≥ (3d+2)/4 (Heath-Brown 2006, Theorem 16), so d ≥ 10;</li>
<li>k ≥ (3d+1)/4 (Browning 2011), so d ≥ 9;</li>
<li>for binomials x<sup>d</sup>+c only, k ≥ (5d+3)/9 (Heath-Brown 2013), so k = d−2 for d ≥ 6. Heath-Brown wrote there that "the most interesting open question then concerns quartic polynomials taking square-free values";</li>
<li>every case, conditionally on the abc conjecture (Granville 1998).</li>
</ul>
<p>Our July report on #978 (<a href="/report/978">/report/978</a>) found the problem open, counted 75,670 squarefree values of n<sup>4</sup>+2 for n ≤ 10<sup>5</sup> with every factorisation checked independently, enclosed the predicted density between 0.75668370 and 0.75668401, and found a sign error in the decisive step of the one public claim then on record (Carella, arXiv 2310.16952).</p>

<p><strong>The claim.</strong> OpenAI, "Squarefree values of quartics and power-free values of polynomials" (dated 24 September 2026, 55 pages, public since 6 October as family 020 of github.com/openai/math). Theorem 1.1: let f ∈ ℤ[x] be irreducible over ℚ of degree d with 4 ≤ d ≤ 8, put k = d − 2, and suppose ρ<sub>f</sub>(p<sup>k</sup>) &lt; p<sup>k</sup> for every prime p (no prime k-th power divides every value). Then</p>
<p style="text-align:center">#{1 ≤ n ≤ X : f(n) is k-power-free} = c<sub>f,k</sub>X + o(X),  c<sub>f,k</sub> = ∏<sub>p</sub>(1 − ρ<sub>f</sub>(p<sup>k</sup>)/p<sup>k</sup>) &gt; 0.</p>
<p>No primitivity or sign condition is needed. Corollary 1.2 extends this to every d ≥ 4, citing Browning (in Xiao's form) for d ≥ 9. Corollary 1.3 does the same for products and families of polynomials whose irreducible factors have degree at most k+2. Everything rests on Proposition 1.4, the large-prime tail: #{X &lt; n ≤ 2X : p<sup>k</sup> | f(n) for some prime p &gt; X} ≪ X<sup>1−δ</sup>.</p>
<p>For #978 this answers both parts that were open. Every irreducible quartic with no fixed square divisor takes squarefree values with the predicted positive density; n<sup>4</sup>+2 is one (Eisenstein at 2, and f(0) = 2 is divisible by no prime square). The (k−2)-question is answered for every k ≥ 4 with density, not just infinitude, and without the page's k ≠ 2<sup>l</sup> or its positive leading coefficient.</p>

<p><strong>The argument.</strong> The paper follows Reuss's framework for power-free values (number-field factorisation plus approximate determinants), with new pieces.</p>
<ul>
<li>§2 reduces the density to Proposition 1.4: a finite sieve for small primes, the trivial bound ρ(p<sup>k</sup>) ≤ d for primes up to X, and the tail for primes above X. The limit in X is taken before the sieve cut-off goes to infinity.</li>
<li>§3 proves a uniform affine counting theorem. It counts integer points of bounded weighted size on algebraic sets whose equations may change from box to box, given lower bounds on the weighted Hilbert functions of their subvarieties. Its proof cuts residue classes at auxiliary primes and uses a random deletion argument for the primes at which later, moving equations fail to give smooth local coordinates.</li>
<li>§4 factors the ideal (n−θ) in K = ℚ(θ): α<sup>k</sup>β = μ(n−θ), with balanced conjugates of sizes P<sup>1/d</sup> and X<sup>b/d</sup>. Integer coordinates of α and β fill two boxes.</li>
<li>§§5–9 cut the paired boxes by archimedean determinants, change to reduced lattice coordinates, and compute weighted Hilbert bounds. They add explicit surface and curve geometry for quartics. On a curve the extreme case is a quintic polynomial with four prescribed critical values, and its integer values form one set of size O(X<sup>1/5</sup>) that is deleted once, before any box is counted (Proposition 8.2).</li>
<li>§10 and Appendix A choose the parameters, and an exact finite certificate checks the exponent margins. §11 assembles the tail: a direct count on the surface f(x) = yz<sup>k</sup> for large η = log p/log X (§11.1), and the number-field count elsewhere (§11.2). The worst margins are 24979/25000 and 3122/3125.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>The Lean, and our replay.</strong> OpenAI's comparator challenge (<code>ComparatorChallenges/PowerFreeValues.lean</code>) states <code>OAI.QuarticPowerFree.allDegrees</code>: for every f ∈ ℤ[x] irreducible over ℚ with natDegree ≥ 4 and <code>LocallyAdmissible f (natDegree − 2)</code>, the local factors are multipliable, their product is positive, and count − c·X = o(X) as real X → ∞. That is Corollary 1.2. The solution, <code>OAI.NumberTheory.PowerFree.Main</code>, is 63 modules and 23,269 lines, and imports nothing but Mathlib.</p>
<p>We compiled all 63 from source on our machine with Lean v4.34.1 and Mathlib d13f23b7 (the repository's pins), module by module, with no cache for anything but Mathlib: 63 of 63, 0 errors, 0 warnings, 17 minutes of wall time on four workers, peak 6.8 GiB per process. The checks on the result:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>#print axioms</code> under <code>--trust=0</code> gives exactly propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound for <code>allDegrees</code>.</li>
<li>The fully elaborated (<code>pp.all</code>) printouts of <code>allDegrees</code> and of the seven definitions it uses (PowerFree, rho, count, LocallyAdmissible, localFactor, densityConstant, DensityStatement) match the comparator's character for character after collapsing whitespace: 8 of 8. A mutation test of the matching script caught all eight mutants: a product in place of the power in PowerFree, Ico in place of Icc in the count, big-O in place of little-o, degree 5 in place of 4, an added axiom, the challenge's sorry removed, a missing block and a missing file.</li>
<li>A planted <code>sorry</code> surfaces as <code>sorryAx</code>.</li>
<li>A scan of the 63 files and the challenge for <code>sorry</code>, <code>axiom</code>, <code>native_decide</code>, <code>implemented_by</code>, <code>extern</code>, <code>unsafe</code>, <code>opaque</code>, meta code and <code>set_option</code> finds only the challenge's own <code>sorry</code>. There is not a single <code>set_option</code> in the closure.</li>
</ul>
<p>LEANCHECKER_SENTENCE</p>
<p>Seat W searched GitHub, the aggregators and the forums and found no earlier report of anyone replaying this formalization. Formal Conjectures marked the problem solved after a build and a source inspection (PR #6898), and conjectures.io closed it on the instruction "Assume the proofs are valid". As far as we can find, this is the first reported kernel replay.</p>

<p><strong>The Lean does not follow the paper.</strong> Seat L read all 63 files against the paper. The closure has no comments, so its map of files to sections is by content. The kernel-checked proof shares with the paper the sieve transfer (§2), the balanced factorisation of Lemma 4.1, an affine counting theorem of Theorem 3.1's shape, weighted Hilbert thresholds of §7's shape, and quartic surface and curve alternatives in the spirit of §§8–9. Then it takes a shorter route:</p>
<ul>
<li>It never makes an archimedean determinant cut. It applies the counting theorem directly to the whole (d+1)-dimensional norm variety in ℤ<sup>2d</sup> (coordinates of α and β in a fixed integral basis, equations a<sub>i</sub><sup>k</sup>b<sub>i</sub> − a<sub>0</sub><sup>k</sup>b<sub>0</sub> = θ<sub>0</sub> − θ<sub>i</sub>). This needs a top-dimensional threshold the paper never states.</li>
<li>It deals with exceptional fibres arithmetically. A fixed n gives one point. A fixed α gives at most a bounded number, since ρ(p<sup>k</sup>) ≤ d and p<sup>k</sup> &gt; X. For "radial" fibres α′ = cα, prime norms force c = ±1.</li>
<li>For 5 ≤ d ≤ 8 it uses one box for the whole range of η, with exponents U = 1/3 and V = 2/5. For d = 4 it uses 100 bins in η with a certificate checked by kernel <code>decide</code>. L re-checked every certificate inequality in exact arithmetic; the tightest margin is 0.00059. The tail saving is X<sup>−3/1000</sup> for 4 ≤ d ≤ 8.</li>
<li>It proves d ≥ 9 itself, by the paper's own §11.1 device: the surface f(x) = yz<sup>k</sup> in two weight boxes, with saving X<sup>−1/2000</sup>. No Browning, Xiao, Heath-Brown or Reuss result enters anywhere. L notes that the paper could have done the same, since on [1, d/k] its §11.1 condition η(1 − kη/d) ≤ 0.2495 is worst at η = 1, where it is 2/d ≤ 2/9.</li>
</ul>
<p>So the Lean needs none of Lemma 4.2, §§5–6, Proposition 8.2 (the quintic deletion), Proposition 10.1, Appendix A, or Browning's theorem. Two things follow. If the replay is right, no objection to those parts of the paper can sink Theorem 1.1, Corollary 1.2 or #978. And the Lean cannot vouch for them either: they stand on the paper alone, as do Corollary 1.3 and §12, which have no formal counterpart.</p>

<p><strong>Formal Conjectures' three statements of #978, proved in Lean (ours).</strong> Formal Conjectures' <code>Erdos978.erdos_978.parts.ii</code> (infinitely many (k−2)-power-free values) and <code>parts.iii</code> ({n | Squarefree (n<sup>4</sup>+2)} is infinite) have been tagged "research solved" since PR #6898 (merged 11 October), with a link to OpenAI's <code>allDegrees</code>, but their proofs are still <code>sorry</code>. So is <code>variants.sub_two</code> (Browning's d ≥ 9 case). Our file <code>Bridge978.lean</code> proves verbatim copies of all three from OpenAI's theorem; a script checks the copies against FC at a7b87c8d, and a mutated copy fails the check. The steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>Irreducible in ℤ[X] with positive degree gives primitive, and Gauss's lemma gives irreducible over ℚ.</li>
<li>FC's local condition (some n ≥ 0 with p<sup>k</sup> ∤ f(n), for every p) gives ρ(p<sup>k</sup>) &lt; p<sup>k</sup> by reducing n mod p<sup>k</sup>.</li>
<li>A positive density makes the count unbounded, so the set is infinite.</li>
<li>OpenAI's PowerFree (no prime k-th power divides a) and FC's Powerfree (x<sup>k</sup> | a forces x to be a unit) are the same set. For <code>parts.iii</code>, X<sup>4</sup>+2 is proved irreducible by Eisenstein at 2, local admissibility comes from f(0) = 2, and Squarefree in ℕ is matched to PowerFree 2 in ℤ.</li>
</ul>
<p>The file also proves the density form in FC's own vocabulary, which FC states only for Hooley's (k−1) case (<code>parts.i</code>): {n | Powerfree (d−2) (f(n))} has natural density (FC's <code>Set.HasDensity</code>, counting 0 ≤ n &lt; b) equal to OpenAI's Euler product, and so has positive density. It needs no k ≠ 2<sup>l</sup> and no sign condition. That matters because FC's <code>parts.ii</code> keeps the page's preamble k ≠ 2<sup>l</sup>, which read literally removes degrees 4 and 8 from part (ii); quartics enter FC only through <code>parts.iii</code>.</p>
<p>All seven bridge theorems depend only on propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound under <code>--trust=0</code>. Three mutants behave as they should:</p>
<ul>
<li>with the conclusion's exponent d−2 changed to d−3, the proof fails at the step that uses it;</li>
<li>with the local condition dropped, it fails where it tries to supply local admissibility (that statement is false: DeepMind's degree-6 polynomial is a counterexample);</li>
<li>with a <code>sorry</code> planted in the density-to-infinitude lemma, <code>sorryAx</code> appears in the axioms of all three FC theorems.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>The reading.</strong> Four seats, all Claude Opus 5.5, were each briefed to refute, to quote the sentence they objected to by the paper's printed numbering, and to grade every objection FATAL, GAP, ERROR or EXPOSITION. Seat M1 took §§2–4, §10 with Appendix A, and §11; seat M2 §§5–9 and §12; seat L the Lean against the paper; seat W the sources and the world. None found a FATAL error or a gap in Theorem 1.1, Corollary 1.2 or anything #978 needs. M1, M2 and L returned HOLDS AS READ. W returned HOLDS AS READ on the Erdős sourcing, the cited ranges and the priority record, and one minor GAP, in Corollary 1.3 (below).</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>M1 recomputed the certificate of §10 and Appendix A in exact arithmetic,</strong> independently of the paper's own listing (which also runs and passes). The cells of width 1/3000 in η are closed, contiguous and cover η = 1 up to the stopping point; the direct-surface range of §11.1 covers the rest up to d/k, including b = 0 and the O(1/log X) overshoot. Every margin is strictly positive. The tightest exponent gap is 0.0019350 (d = 4, cell 3555, η ∈ [1.18500, 1.18533]; Table 1 rounds it down to 0.00191). One cut inequality of (10.4) holds by a relative margin of only 1.5·10<sup>−8</sup> (d = 4, cell 4902), because w is the least grid point that works; it is strict, so valid, but the "sufficiently large" in Proposition 5.2 is enormous there, which costs nothing since no effective δ is claimed. Six mutations each make M1's checker fail: a gap of 10<sup>−6</sup> in one cell, a raised stopping threshold, a weaker top-dimensional threshold, w shrunk 1% in one cell, and two that matter for the route — the quartic curve constant g = 4 replaced by 3 (no quintic deletion) breaks 434 cells, and the quartic surface bound 3/U<sup>2</sup> replaced by 2/U<sup>2</sup> breaks 609. So the paper's quartic route really does need Propositions 8.1 and 8.2. The Lean uses g = 3 and does not need them, because it counts on a different variety.</li>
<li><strong>M1 on the saving.</strong> The paper never computes δ in Proposition 1.4. From its own certificate it is at most about 5·10<sup>−4</sup>, set by the direct range (1/2 + √(b′η′/d) reaches 0.99954 for d = 8 and 0.99943 for d = 4), not by Table 1's gaps. The Lean's 3/1000 is larger than anything the paper's route gives; the two agree only in being positive.</li>
<li><strong>M1 on §§2–4.</strong> The sieve transfer takes its limits in a valid order (cut-off fixed, X → ∞, then the cut-off, then the dyadic pieces); (2.2) is right for every e ≥ 1 and local admissibility forces e &lt; k; the tail is the primitive part's once X exceeds the content primes. Every constant inside §3 is independent of the coefficients of the moving varieties: Theorem 3.1 pushes all the uniformity into its hypotheses, so the weakest link is whether Propositions 7.1, 8.1 and 9.2 hold uniformly for every subvariety in moving coordinates (M2's ground). The box sizes of §4 depend only on f, and the "at most k<sup>d</sup> local branches" count holds. Expository: §11 needs its dyadic intervals anchored at P = 2<sup>i</sup>X, or the first η falls just below 1, where no cell of the main theorem applies.</li>
<li><strong>M2 read §§5–9 and checked every concrete claim by computation.</strong> Both determinant cuts in §5 take a kernel vector against a basis of the component's own coordinate space, so the form they produce cannot vanish identically on that component; and the split into mixed and pure equations is exhaustive, because the bidegree is never (0,0). The Hilbert bounds are bounds on the variety actually present: random-point evaluation ranks modulo a prime equal the paper's complete-intersection series exactly for the bicone of Lemma 5.1 (10 bidegrees, d = 4 and 5), a saturated first-cut component (5 bidegrees) and three examples of Proposition 7.1's pair image under five weightings (15 runs), with no mismatch; in each, the unsaturated weight would have given the wrong count, so the saturation step is needed and right. The surface case analysis of §8.1 (a-image of degree ≥ 3, 2 or 1) is exhaustive, and in the plane case both alternatives the paper rules out are genuinely impossible. Uniformity in moving equations, coordinate changes and boxes is argued at every place it is used.</li>
<li><strong>M2 on the quintic set (Proposition 8.2).</strong> An exact resultant computation finds exactly 25 normalised quintics with critical values (1, i, −1, −i), 25 for a reordering and 25 for generic values (0, 1, 3, 7) — the Hurwitz count 5<sup>5−3</sup>. The roots of x<sup>4</sup>+1 and x<sup>4</sup>+2 reduce to (1, i, −1, −i) after scaling. The critical values are the roots of f, fixed before any box, curve or prime is chosen, so the set of their integer values (O(X<sup>1/5</sup>) of them) is one global set and deleting it once is legitimate. The paper says only "finitely many"; stating 25 would let a reader check it.</li>
<li><strong>M2 on §12</strong> (the worked example n<sup>4</sup>+1): consistent with the general argument. Its enlarged-cell certificate passes in exact arithmetic (gap − 4τ = 1941/10<sup>5</sup>), ℤ[ζ<sub>8</sub>] is norm-Euclidean by the rounding argument given, and the Pell estimate holds.</li>
<li><strong>One disagreement, on Corollary 1.3 only.</strong> For d = k+1 the proof cites Reuss's Lemma 3, its proof and his §6 for the triple count. W graded this a GAP: Reuss's §§2–3 work in ℤ[θ] with θ integral, i.e. for monic f, and the paper applies them to a primitive, possibly non-monic g without the standard reduction (substitute y = cn, so that c<sup>d−1</sup>g(y/c) is monic). M1 graded it EXPOSITION: Reuss's statement is for general irreducible f, and the estimate is assembled from his proof text rather than stated. Either way it is fillable, it touches only Corollary 1.3, and Corollary 1.3 has no Lean.</li>
<li><strong>Smaller points.</strong> The paper says Zapata Ceballos–Jalalvand's class "includes n<sup>4</sup>+1"; it included n<sup>4</sup>+2 too (W). Booker–Browning 2016 is not cited for the k = 2 case of Corollary 1.3 (W). The Erdős history omits [Er81h] (W). Xiao's Theorem 9.1 needs no primitivity step, and the estimate used from Xiao's §9 sits inside a proof sketch (M1, W).</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Numbers (computer-assisted, label (d); they test nothing about the theorem).</strong> For f = x<sup>4</sup>+2, ρ(p<sup>2</sup>) is 2 for p ≡ 3 mod 8, 4 or 0 for p ≡ 1 mod 8, and 0 otherwise (our July report proves this, and the formula is checked by brute force for p ≤ 10<sup>4</sup>). The predicted density is c ≈ 0.7566840, enclosed rigorously in July between 0.75668370 and 0.75668401; for x<sup>4</sup>+1 it is c<sub>8</sub> = ∏<sub>p≡1 (8)</sub>(1 − 4/p<sup>2</sup>) ≈ 0.9808175. The counts:</p>
<table style="margin:1em 0;border-collapse:collapse"><thead><tr><th style="padding:2px 10px">N</th><th style="padding:2px 10px">#{n ≤ N : n<sup>4</sup>+2 squarefree}</th><th style="padding:2px 10px">share</th><th style="padding:2px 10px">#{n ≤ N : n<sup>4</sup>+1 squarefree}</th><th style="padding:2px 10px">share</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">10<sup>5</sup></td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">75,670</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">0.75670</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">98,090</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">0.98090</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">2·10<sup>5</sup></td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">151,346</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">0.75673</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">196,180</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">0.98090</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">10<sup>6</sup></td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">756,674</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">0.756674</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">980,831</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">0.980831</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">limit</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right"></td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">0.756684</td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right"></td><td style="padding:2px 10px;text-align:right">0.980817</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Each count was made twice, by different methods. The first factors every n<sup>4</sup>+w in PARI/GP: our July run to 10<sup>5</sup>, whose factorisations a separate program certified; today's run to 2·10<sup>5</sup>; and seat M2's to 10<sup>6</sup>. The second factors nothing. It sieves with every prime p ≤ 10<sup>6</sup>, marking the classes n ≡ r mod p<sup>2</sup> for each root r of x<sup>4</sup>+w lifted from p to p<sup>2</sup>. The two agree at every checkpoint. A version of the sieve with the lift removed gives 65,353 at 10<sup>5</sup>, so agreement is not automatic.</p>
<p>The large-prime tail that Proposition 1.4 is about is tiny here. Factoring every value up to 10<sup>6</sup> (M2's run; ours to 10<sup>5</sup> agrees) finds only two n with p<sup>2</sup> | n<sup>4</sup>+2 for a prime p &gt; n: n = 2 (p = 3) and n = 97 (97<sup>4</sup>+2 = 179<sup>2</sup>·2763). For n<sup>4</sup>+1 there are none. The sieve is exact for N ≤ 10<sup>6</sup> only because of this census: a prime p &gt; 10<sup>6</sup> with p<sup>2</sup> | f(n) and n ≤ 10<sup>6</sup> would have p &gt; n, and none does.
</p>

<p><strong>Sources and the world.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Erdős's three sentences</strong> are quoted above from the page images of [Er53] (users.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1953-02.pdf), [Er65b] (1965-17.pdf) and [Er81h] (1981-33.pdf), read by seat W. The paper cites the first two, not [Er81h].</li>
<li><strong>The two earlier claims did not hold up.</strong> Zapata Ceballos and Jalalvand (arXiv 2608.10335, v1 11 August 2026) claimed positive squarefree density for monic irreducible degree-2q polynomials whose field has a degree-q Galois subfield. Their Corollary 2.4 covers every monic quartic whose Galois group is neither S<sub>4</sub> nor A<sub>4</sub>, so x<sup>4</sup>+2 as well. The paper cites v1 as a claim it does not use, and says it "includes n<sup>4</sup>+1"; it also included n<sup>4</sup>+2. The authors withdrew it on 15 October: "The proof of Proposition 2.3 is incomplete. The argument does not provide the estimate needed to control the contribution of square divisors arising from prime ideals of large norm." That is the large-prime tail, the step Proposition 1.4 is about. Carella's arXiv 2310.16952 (2023, math.GM, one version, unrefereed) claims n<sup>4</sup>+1 and n<sup>4</sup>+2 with a power saving. Our July report found a sign error in its decisive fractional-part estimate. Seat W found its constants wrong as well: 0.757159 and 0.981003 against true values 0.756684 and 0.980817, and its Theorem 1.3 constant 0.9638 where the density is 0.9006.</li>
<li><strong>Uncited prior work.</strong> Booker and Browning (Discrete Analysis 2016:8, Theorem 1.2) already proved the k = 2 case of Corollary 1.3 for factors of degree at most 3, by the same factor-by-factor tail.</li>
<li><strong>The record elsewhere.</strong> The teorth database still lists #978 as open (last update 31 August 2025). The problem page was last edited on 31 March 2026, does not mention OpenAI, lists no proof claims, and has suspended comments. Formal Conjectures marked parts ii and iii solved in PR #6898 (Moritz Firsching, merged 11 October), whose stated checks are a build, link resolution and a grep for <code>sorry</code>, with no kernel replay. Its status-mismatch bot issue #7135 is open. A preprint of 21 October on random polynomials (arXiv 2610.29911) still calls the problem "a deep open problem" beyond degree 3 and does not cite OpenAI.</li>
<li><strong>The paper and its Lean have not changed since release.</strong> The manuscript and <code>Main.lean</code> have the same git hashes at the initial commit (adc7f12, 6 October) and at the current head (1581d4c). FC's link (<code>Main.lean#L144</code>) points at <code>allDegrees</code>.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>What this entry is.</strong> The theorem is OpenAI's, and its proof is OpenAI's model's. What is ours: the replay and its controls; the readings; the Lean bridge proving Formal Conjectures' <code>parts.ii</code>, <code>parts.iii</code> and <code>variants.sub_two</code>, plus the density form in FC's vocabulary; and the numbers. Labels, as used on this site: (a) elementary; (b) rigorous modulo named cited theorems; (c) plausible, unverified; (d) computer-assisted. Theorem 1.1, Corollary 1.2 and the three FC statements are (d): Lean-verified, kernel-replayed here, unrefereed by any human. Corollary 1.3, §12 and the paper's own route through §§5–6, 8.2 and 10 are paper-only: our AI seats read them, as reported above, and nothing formal backs them.</p>
<p><strong>Where to look for a hole.</strong> In the formal claim, only four places can hide one, and they can be read without trusting anything else:</p>
<ol>
<li>the seven definitions in <code>Model.lean</code>, which the comparator repeats (are they the problem?);</li>
<li>the statement of <code>allDegrees</code>;</li>
<li>the kernel (our replay, and the <code>leanchecker</code> pass);</li>
<li>our bridge’s 330 lines, against FC's text.</li>
</ol>
<p>In the paper, the parts the Lean does not touch are the ones to read hardest: Lemma 4.2, §§5–6, Proposition 8.2, the certificate of §10 and Appendix A, and Corollary 1.3.</p>
